.TH "BEAR" "1" "August 16, 2026" "Bear User Manuals"
.SH NAME
bear \- generate a compilation database for Clang tooling
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]bear\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \-\- \f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...
.PP
\f[B]bear intercept\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \-\-output \f[I]FILE\f[R]
\-\- \f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...
.PP
\f[B]bear semantic\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]bear parse\-sh\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Bear produces a JSON compilation database
(\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R]).
Clang\-based tools such as clangd and clang\-tidy read this file to
learn how each source file of a project is compiled: with which
compiler, which flags, from which directory.
Build systems know this, but most do not export it; Bear recovers it
from the build itself.
.PP
Bear works in two stages.
First it captures the commands a build executes, and writes them as a
stream of execution events.
The capture either observes a real build run, or parses the text of a
dry run.
Then semantic analysis recognizes the compiler invocations among those
events, parses their command lines, and writes the compilation database.
.SS Modes
.TP
\f[B]Combined mode\f[R] (default)
\f[CR]bear \-\- <build command>\f[R] runs the build, captures and
analyzes in one step, and writes the database.
This is the recommended way to use Bear: it observes the commands the
build actually executes, so the result is exact.
Use it whenever you can run the build.
.TP
\f[B]Intercept mode\f[R]
\f[CR]bear intercept\f[R] runs the build but only captures, writing the
raw event stream to a file.
Useful when the build is expensive and you want to run it once and
process the events several times, for example to try different output
configurations.
.TP
\f[B]Semantic mode\f[R]
\f[CR]bear semantic\f[R] runs no build: it is a filter that reads an
event stream (from \f[CR]bear intercept\f[R], or any producer of the
documented event format) and writes the compilation database.
Combine it with intercept mode to re\-generate the database without
rebuilding.
.TP
\f[B]Parse\-sh mode\f[R]
\f[CR]bear parse\-sh\f[R] produces the compilation database from shell
command text, without executing anything.
Typical input is a build system\(cqs dry run (\f[CR]make \-n\f[R]) or a
saved build log.
It is a text parser and inherently lower fidelity than interception;
reach for it when the build cannot be run, for example to recover a
database from a CI log.
See its limitations under COMMANDS.
.SS Interception methods
Interception itself has two methods, selectable in the configuration
file.
\f[B]Preload\f[R] (the default on Linux and the BSDs) injects a small
library into every process the build starts and observes its
\f[CR]exec()\f[R] calls.
\f[B]Wrapper\f[R] (the default on macOS and Windows) substitutes the
known compilers with a reporting wrapper executable.
Preload is transparent to the build but cannot observe statically linked
executables, and it is unavailable on Windows and on macOS while System
Integrity Protection is enabled; forcing it there is a startup error
that names wrapper mode, not a silent fallback.
Wrapper mode works on every platform, but the build has to pick the
wrapper up: a \(lqconfigure\(rq step that discovers compilers must
itself run under Bear (see TROUBLESHOOTING).
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-c, \-\-config\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the configuration file.
It controls the interception method, compiler recognition, source
filtering, duplicate handling, and output formatting (see
CONFIGURATION).
Given before a subcommand it applies to that subcommand too.
.TP
\f[B]\-o, \-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the compilation database to write (default:
\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R]).
\f[CR]\-\f[R] is not accepted here, because in combined mode standard
output belongs to the build.
To stream the database, use \f[CR]bear semantic \-\-output \-\f[R] (see
COMMANDS).
.TP
\f[B]\-a, \-\-append\f[R]
Append to an existing output file instead of overwriting it.
New entries are placed before the existing ones, so a rebuilt file\(cqs
newest invocation survives duplicate filtering and replaces the stale
entry (see \f[CR]duplicates\f[R] under CONFIGURATION).
.TP
\f[B]\-h, \-\-help\f[R]
Print help.
.TP
\f[B]\-V, \-\-version\f[R]
Print version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS bear intercept
Runs the build and captures execution events to a file, without
analyzing them.
.PP
\f[B]bear intercept\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] \-\-output \f[I]FILE\f[R]
\-\- \f[I]BUILD_COMMAND\f[R]\&...
.TP
\f[B]\-o, \-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the event file to write.
Required, and \f[CR]\-\f[R] is not accepted: the build owns standard
output, and there is no default event\-file name.
.SS bear semantic
Reads an event stream and writes the compilation database.
It runs no build, so it behaves as a plain filter: diagnostics go to
standard error, and both ends can be standard streams.
.PP
\f[B]bear semantic\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.TP
\f[B]\-i, \-\-input\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the event file to read (default: \f[CR]\-\f[R], standard input).
Any producer of a conforming event stream can pipe into it.
When standard input is a terminal, or the stream turns out to be empty,
a notice is printed on standard error.
.TP
\f[B]\-o, \-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the compilation database to write (default:
\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R]).
Pass \f[CR]\-\f[R] to write to standard output; that write is neither
atomic nor appendable, so \f[CR]\-\-output \-\f[R] together with
\f[CR]\-\-append\f[R] is rejected.
.TP
\f[B]\-a, \-\-append\f[R]
Same as in combined mode: place new entries before the existing ones in
the output file.
.SS bear parse\-sh
Produces the compilation database from shell command text, without
running anything.
Typical input is a build system\(cqs dry\-run output, such as
\f[CR]make \-n\f[R], or a saved build log:
.IP
.EX
make \-n | bear parse\-sh
.EE
.PP
\f[B]bear parse\-sh\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]]
.TP
\f[B]\-i, \-\-input\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the shell text to parse (default: \f[CR]\-\f[R], standard
input).
.TP
\f[B]\-o, \-\-output\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]
Path of the compilation database to write (default:
\f[CR]compile_commands.json\f[R]).
Pass \f[CR]\-\f[R] to write to standard output; that write is neither
atomic nor appendable, so \f[CR]\-\-output \-\f[R] together with
\f[CR]\-\-append\f[R] is rejected.
.TP
\f[B]\-a, \-\-append\f[R]
Same as in combined mode: place new entries before the existing ones in
the output file.
.TP
\f[B]\-C, \-\-directory\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R]
Initial working directory for the parsed commands.
Use it for input captured elsewhere (a CI log, a dry run from another
checkout).
Give an absolute path; the directory need not exist on this machine.
.PP
The commands recognized in the text go through the same semantic
analysis and output stage as an intercepted build, so the configuration
applies exactly as in combined mode.
Parsed commands that are not compiler invocations (\f[CR]ar\f[R],
\f[CR]mv\f[R], \f[CR]mkdir\f[R]) simply produce no entries.
.PP
It understands a documented subset of POSIX shell syntax: word splitting
and quoting; the \f[CR];\f[R], \f[CR]&&\f[R], \f[CR]||\f[R],
\f[CR]&\f[R], and \f[CR]|\f[R] separators; comments; redirections;
\f[CR]cd\f[R]; brace groups (\f[CR]{ ...; }\f[R], whose \f[CR]cd\f[R]
persists past the closing brace); and make\(cqs
\f[CR]Entering directory\f[R] / \f[CR]Leaving directory\f[R] markers,
which track the working directory across a recursive build.
Sub\-makes print these markers by default; run \f[CR]make \-nw\f[R] to
have the top\-level make print them too, so the log itself names the
build root.
A line using anything outside that subset is skipped and reported on
standard error with its line number and reason.
This covers subshells, command substitution, parameter expansion, globs
in the executable position, here\-documents, unterminated quotes, and
shell keywords such as \f[CR]if\f[R] or \f[CR]for\f[R].
The run succeeds as long as at least one line parsed as a command.
.PP
Interception remains the higher\-fidelity default: it observes the
\f[CR]exec()\f[R] calls a build really makes, while
\f[CR]bear parse\-sh\f[R] reconstructs approximate events from text the
build system chose to print.
A dry run can omit commands (recursive make does not always propagate
\f[CR]\-n\f[R], and commands behind not\-yet\-generated sources never
print); the environment and \f[CR]PATH\f[R] used to resolve bare
executable names are the parse\-time ones, not the real build\(cqs; and
only POSIX \f[CR]sh\f[R] text is supported.
Prefer \f[CR]bear \-\- <build command>\f[R] whenever the build can
actually be run.
.PP
The source files named in the parsed commands need not exist: entries
are reconstructed from the text alone.
The one exception is the \f[CR]canonical\f[R] path format, which
resolves symlinks on disk; when the sources are absent, configure
\f[CR]absolute\f[R] instead (see \f[CR]format.paths\f[R] under
CONFIGURATION).
.SH OUTPUT
Bear writes a JSON compilation database conforming to the Clang JSON
Compilation Database specification (see SEE ALSO): a JSON array of entry
objects with the following fields.
.TP
\f[B]directory\f[R]
The working directory of the compilation.
.TP
\f[B]file\f[R]
The main translation unit source file.
.TP
\f[B]arguments\f[R]
The compilation command as an array of strings.
This is the default; Bear prefers it over \f[CR]command\f[R] because it
avoids shell\-escaping issues.
.TP
\f[B]command\f[R]
The compilation command as a single shell\-escaped string (written
instead of \f[CR]arguments\f[R] when
\f[CR]format.entries.use_array_format\f[R] is \f[CR]false\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]output\f[R]
The file produced by the compilation (optional).
.PP
By default Bear does not transform paths: each entry records them as
they appeared in the intercepted invocation, so \f[CR]file\f[R] and
\f[CR]output\f[R] are often relative to \f[CR]directory\f[R].
Use \f[CR]format.paths\f[R] in the configuration to normalize them.
.PP
Two compilers produce an entry shape worth knowing about:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]swiftc\f[R]: a whole\-module invocation naming several
\f[CR].swift\f[R] sources produces one entry per source, each carrying
the complete invocation\(cqs arguments (every source of the module, not
just its own).
This matches the shape CMake emits and SourceKit\-LSP consumes: in
whole\-module compilation each file\(cqs semantics depend on every other
source.
The repeated argument data is expected, not a bug.
The internal \f[CR]swift\-frontend\f[R] jobs that \f[CR]swiftc\f[R]
spawns are filtered out automatically.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]valac\f[R]: one entry per \f[CR]valac\f[R] invocation (valac
compiles all of a target\(cqs \f[CR].vala\f[R]/\f[CR].gs\f[R] sources
together as one unit); the entry\(cqs \f[CR]file\f[R] is the first
source and every source is kept in the command.
Since valac transpiles to C and compiles the result, the database also
contains entries for the generated C files (see TROUBLESHOOTING for the
clangd consequences).
.SH CONFIGURATION
Bear reads a YAML configuration file, found by search (see FILES) or
named with \f[CR]\-\-config\f[R].
Without one, built\-in defaults apply.
A file that is written at all must name \f[CR]schema\f[R]; every section
below it is optional.
The example below shows every section:
.IP
.EX
schema\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq4.2\(dq
intercept\f[B]:\f[R]
  mode\f[B]:\f[R] wrapper\f[I]              # default: preload on Linux and the BSDs\f[R]
compilers\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/bin/cc
    as\f[B]:\f[R] gcc
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/local/bin/gcc
    ignore\f[B]:\f[R] true
sources\f[B]:\f[R]
  directories\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /project/tests
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
  files\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dqmoc_*.cpp\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.cc\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
duplicates\f[B]:\f[R]
  match_on\f[B]:\f[R]\f[I]                  # default: directory, file\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] file
    \f[B]\-\f[R] arguments
format\f[B]:\f[R]
  paths\f[B]:\f[R]
    directory\f[B]:\f[R] canonical\f[I]     # default: as\-is\f[R]
    file\f[B]:\f[R] canonical\f[I]          # default: as\-is\f[R]
  entries\f[B]:\f[R]
    use_array_format\f[B]:\f[R] true
    include_output_field\f[B]:\f[R] true
  arguments\f[B]:\f[R]
    from_response_files\f[B]:\f[R] false
    from_environment\f[B]:\f[R] true
headers\f[B]:\f[R]
  enabled\f[B]:\f[R] true\f[I]               # default: false\f[R]
  strategy\f[B]:\f[R] siblings
.EE
.PP
Every value above is illustrative; the non\-default ones are annotated
inline.
Each section below states its actual defaults.
.SS intercept
Controls the interception method (see DESCRIPTION for the trade\-offs):
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]mode\f[R]: \f[CR]preload\f[R] (default on Linux and the BSDs) or
\f[CR]wrapper\f[R] (default on macOS and Windows; works everywhere).
.SS compilers
Hints for compiler recognition.
Bear recognizes GCC\- and Clang\-compatible drivers (including
cross\-compiler prefixed names), the common vendor compiler drivers and
standalone assemblers, \f[CR]swiftc\f[R] and \f[CR]valac\f[R], compiler
launchers, and MPI compiler wrappers.
Run \f[CR]bear semantic \-\-print\-compilers\f[R] for the full list of
recognized compiler names, each with the \f[CR]as\f[R] value it maps to.
.PP
A few recognition behaviors are worth knowing.
Compiler launchers (\f[CR]ccache\f[R], \f[CR]distcc\f[R],
\f[CR]sccache\f[R], \f[CR]icecc\f[R]) are dropped from the recorded
command: \f[CR]ccache gcc \-c main.c\f[R] produces an entry for
\f[CR]gcc \-c main.c\f[R].
MPI compiler wrappers are recorded as invoked, not expanded to the
compiler they wrap; Clang tooling that needs the wrapper\(cqs baked\-in
include paths can point at it directly, for example with clangd\(cqs
\f[CR]\-\-query\-driver\f[R].
C++20 module\-interface units (\f[CR].cppm\f[R], \f[CR].ixx\f[R], and
similar) are recognized as sources, while precompiled \f[CR].pcm\f[R]
artifacts never appear as an entry\(cqs \f[CR]file\f[R].
.PP
In wrapper mode this section also decides what gets wrapped.
With no entry other than \f[CR]ignore\f[R] ones, Bear scans PATH at
startup and wraps every compiler it recognizes.
As soon as one entry is not ignored, Bear wraps exactly the listed paths
and skips the scan, so listing one unusual compiler stops \f[CR]gcc\f[R]
and \f[CR]clang\f[R] from being intercepted unless they are listed too.
Compilers named in the compiler environment variables (\f[CR]CC\f[R],
\f[CR]CXX\f[R], and similar) are wrapped either way.
Preload mode is unaffected: it intercepts every executed program
regardless of this section.
.PP
Use this section when a compiler at a given path is not recognized, or
is recognized wrongly:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]path\f[R]: Path of the compiler executable.
Required.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]as\f[R]: Compiler type hint for semantic analysis.
Optional; when omitted, Bear guesses the family from the executable\(cqs
filename and falls back to \f[CR]gcc\f[R] when nothing matches.
The accepted values are the \f[CR]as\f[R] names shown by
\f[CR]bear semantic \-\-print\-compilers\f[R], plus \f[CR]wrapper\f[R]
for a compiler launcher.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]ignore\f[R]: Exclude this executable\(cqs invocations from the
database.
Optional, default \f[CR]false\f[R].
.PP
The generic names \f[CR]cc\f[R], \f[CR]c++\f[R], and the HPE Cray PrgEnv
wrapper \f[CR]CC\f[R] are classified by probing the executable\(cqs
\f[CR]\-\-version\f[R] output, since the same basename can be a
different compiler depending on the platform or the loaded environment
module.
When the probe cannot classify the executable, override it:
.IP
.EX
compilers\f[B]:\f[R]
  \f[B]\-\f[R] path\f[B]:\f[R] /usr/bin/cc
    as\f[B]:\f[R] clang
.EE
.SS sources
Filters entries by source file location and filename.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]directories\f[R]: List of directory\-based rules, each with a
\f[B]path\f[R] and an \f[B]action\f[R] (\f[CR]include\f[R] or
\f[CR]exclude\f[R]).
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]files\f[R]: List of filename\-glob rules, each with a
\f[B]pattern\f[R] and an \f[B]action\f[R] (\f[CR]include\f[R] or
\f[CR]exclude\f[R]).
.PP
Rules of each list are evaluated in order, the last matching rule wins,
and an entry matched by no rule is included; an empty list includes
everything.
The two lists compose: an entry is emitted only when both accept it.
A file pattern without a path separator matches the source file\(cqs
basename; a pattern containing a separator matches the full source path
as it appears in the entry, so use the same path format as configured in
\f[CR]format.paths.file\f[R].
An invalid pattern is rejected during configuration validation with an
error naming the offending rule.
Typical use for file patterns is dropping machine\-generated sources (Qt
\f[CR]moc\f[R] output, protobuf stubs) so that linters and editors act
only on hand\-written code; see EXAMPLES.
.SS duplicates
Controls duplicate detection.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]match_on\f[R]: List of entry fields to compare (\f[CR]file\f[R],
\f[CR]arguments\f[R], \f[CR]directory\f[R], \f[CR]command\f[R],
\f[CR]output\f[R]).
Two entries are duplicates when all of the configured fields match; the
first occurrence is kept.
The default is \f[CR]directory\f[R] and \f[CR]file\f[R], so one entry is
kept per source file per directory, regardless of arguments; when a
build compiles the same file with different flags, add
\f[CR]arguments\f[R] to keep an entry per configuration.
Combined with \f[CR]\-\-append\f[R] (new entries first), the default
means a rebuilt file\(cqs newest invocation replaces its stale entry.
.SS format
Controls output formatting.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]paths.directory\f[R], \f[B]paths.file\f[R]: How to format these
fields\(cq paths: \f[CR]as\-is\f[R] (default, no transformation),
\f[CR]canonical\f[R] (resolve symlinks; requires the path to exist),
\f[CR]relative\f[R] (to the \f[CR]directory\f[R] field), or
\f[CR]absolute\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]entries.use_array_format\f[R]: Use the \f[CR]arguments\f[R] array
(default) instead of the \f[CR]command\f[R] string.
Set to \f[CR]false\f[R] for consumers that read only the
\f[CR]command\f[R] field.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]entries.include_output_field\f[R]: Include the \f[CR]output\f[R]
field in entries.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]arguments.from_response_files\f[R]: Replace \f[CR]\(atfile\f[R]
response\-file references with the file\(cqs tokenized contents
(resolved relative to the compilation\(cqs working directory, expanded
recursively, using the compiler\(cqs quoting convention).
Disabled by default, in which case an \f[CR]\(atfile\f[R] argument is
recorded verbatim; a missing or unreadable file is left literal with a
warning.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]arguments.from_environment\f[R]: Fold compiler environment
variables that act as implicit flags into the arguments.
GCC/Clang header\-search paths (\f[CR]CPATH\f[R],
\f[CR]C_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R], \f[CR]CPLUS_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R],
\f[CR]OBJC_INCLUDE_PATH\f[R]) become include flags, and MSVC\(cqs
\f[CR]CL\f[R] / \f[CR]_CL_\f[R] become leading / trailing options.
Enabled by default; set to \f[CR]false\f[R] to record only the
command\-line flags.
(Unrelated to the \f[CR]CC=\(dqgcc \-std=c11\(dq\f[R] convention handled
during interception; see TROUBLESHOOTING.)
.SS headers
Editors and linters often need compile flags for header files, not only
for the translation units that were compiled.
When enabled, Bear synthesizes an entry for a header by cloning the
arguments of a compiled C/C++/Objective\-C translation unit, with the
source path replaced by the header path and the output flag removed.
Off by default.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]enabled\f[R]: Turn header\-entry synthesis on or off.
Default \f[CR]false\f[R].
.IP \(bu 2
\f[B]strategy\f[R]: How headers are discovered and which translation
unit donates the flags:
.RS 2
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]siblings\f[R] (default): headers receive an entry cloned from a
compiled source in the same directory.
Zero prerequisites, but the flags are approximate, and headers in
directories without compiled sources (the split \f[CR]include/\f[R] +
\f[CR]src/\f[R] layout) get nothing.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]dependency\-files\f[R]: reads the make\-style \f[CR].d\f[R]
dependency files the build already emitted (for example via
\f[CR]\-MMD\f[R]) and synthesizes an entry for each header prerequisite
that resolves inside the compilation\(cqs working directory.
Accurate, and reaches headers in other directories, but requires the
build to have left dependency files on disk.
.RE
.PP
The header extension set is fixed (the built\-in C\-family headers).
Synthesized entries pass through duplicate detection like any other
entry, so a header with a real compilation entry is not duplicated.
.SH EXIT STATUS
In combined and intercept mode, Bear exits with the build command\(cqs
exit code: 0 when the build succeeds, the same non\-zero code when it
fails.
.PP
In semantic mode, Bear exits 0 on success and non\-zero when the
analysis fails.
.PP
In parse\-sh mode, Bear exits 0 when the input could be parsed \- even
when the database comes out empty because the text named no compiler
invocations \- and also on empty input (with a notice on standard
error).
It exits non\-zero when every non\-empty input line was skipped, so a
run that understood nothing cannot pass for a successful one.
.PP
If Bear itself encounters an internal error, it exits non\-zero
regardless of the build command\(cqs status.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]RUST_LOG\f[R]
Selects the diagnostic format and verbosity on standard error.
When unset, Bear uses the UNIX style, \f[CR]bear: message\f[R] (and
\f[CR]wrapper:\f[R] or \f[CR]preload:\f[R] from its helper processes),
showing warnings and errors only.
When set to a level (\f[CR]error\f[R], \f[CR]warn\f[R], \f[CR]info\f[R],
or \f[CR]debug\f[R], in increasing verbosity), Bear switches to a
developer format that tags every line with a timestamp, the level, the
emitting process and its pid, and the source location.
Debug output is essential for troubleshooting; see TROUBLESHOOTING.
.SH FILES
The configuration file \f[CR]bear.yml\f[R] is searched in the following
locations, in order; the first file found is loaded:
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]./bear.yml\f[B]\f[R]
The current working directory.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/Bear/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Unix)
When \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]$HOME/.config/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]$HOME/.config/Bear/bear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Unix)
When \f[CR]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R] is unset.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]%LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]%LOCALAPPDATA%\(rsBear\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Windows)
When \f[CR]%LOCALAPPDATA%\f[R] is set.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]%APPDATA%\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R], \f[B]\f[CB]%APPDATA%\(rsBear\(rsbear.yml\f[B]\f[R] (Windows)
When \f[CR]%APPDATA%\f[R] is set.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a database for a Make project:
.IP
.EX
bear \-\- make
.EE
.PP
For a CMake project in preload mode, only the build needs Bear:
.IP
.EX
cmake \-B build
bear \-\- cmake \-\-build build
.EE
.PP
In wrapper mode the configure step must also run under Bear, so it
discovers the wrapper as the compiler; discard that run\(cqs output:
.IP
.EX
bear \-\- cmake \-B build
bear \-\- cmake \-\-build build
.EE
.PP
Capture once, analyze many times.
This allows trying output configurations without rebuilding:
.IP
.EX
bear intercept \-\-output events.json \-\- make
bear semantic \-\-input events.json
bear \-\-config strict.yml semantic \-\-input events.json \-\-output strict.json
.EE
.PP
Recover a database from a dry run, without building:
.IP
.EX
make \-n | bear parse\-sh
.EE
.PP
For a recursive Make build, add \f[CR]\-w\f[R].
The top\-level make then prints \f[CR]Entering directory\f[R] markers
too, and parse\-sh resolves every command against the right directory:
.IP
.EX
make \-nw | bear parse\-sh
.EE
.PP
Update the database after rebuilding one part of the project:
.IP
.EX
bear \-\-append \-\- make \-C src/module
.EE
.PP
Drop generated sources (Qt moc output, protobuf stubs) with a
\f[CR]bear.yml\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
sources\f[B]:\f[R]
  files\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dqmoc_*.cpp\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.cc\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
    \f[B]\-\f[R] pattern\f[B]:\f[R] \(dq*.pb.h\(dq
      action\f[B]:\f[R] exclude
.EE
.PP
Synthesize entries for headers in a split \f[CR]include/\f[R] +
\f[CR]src/\f[R] layout, when the build emits \f[CR].d\f[R] dependency
files:
.IP
.EX
headers\f[B]:\f[R]
  enabled\f[B]:\f[R] true
  strategy\f[B]:\f[R] dependency\-files
.EE
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS Debug logging
Before reporting any issue, run Bear with debug logging enabled and
include the output in the report:
.IP
.EX
RUST_LOG=debug bear \-\- <build command>
.EE
.SS Empty output
The most common cause is that the build executed no compiler: an
incremental build with everything up to date runs nothing.
Bear intercepts executed commands only; it does not read the build
files.
Run a clean build.
.PP
In wrapper mode, a \(lqconfigure\(rq step that detects compilers
captures the real compiler path before Bear can substitute the wrapper.
Run the configure step under Bear too (discarding that output), then run
the build under Bear.
.SS Compiler variables with flags
In wrapper mode Bear accepts the GNU Make convention of a compiler
variable carrying a trailing flag or two
(\f[CR]CC=\(dqgcc \-std=c11\(dq make\f[R]): it splits the value on
whitespace, resolves the first token as the compiler, and rewrites the
variable so the build still sees the flags.
This is a Unix / GNU Make convention; native Windows build systems
(MSBuild, \f[CR]nmake\f[R]) do not consume \f[CR]CC\f[R]/\f[CR]CXX\f[R]
from the environment.
For anything beyond simple whitespace\-separated tokens (quoting,
metacharacters, substitutions), use \f[CR]CFLAGS\f[R] /
\f[CR]CXXFLAGS\f[R] / \f[CR]LDFLAGS\f[R] instead; Bear passes those
through untouched.
.SS Preload errors in cross\-compilation
An error like
\f[CR]version \(aqGLIBC_2.33\(aq not found (required by .../libexec.so)\f[R]
means Bear\(cqs preload library was built against a newer glibc than the
one the SDK\(cqs compilers load: the library must be ABI\-compatible
with the libc of the intercepted process, not only with the host\(cqs.
Use a Bear build linked against a glibc no newer than the SDK\(cqs.
The project wiki\(cqs Troubleshooting page lists diagnostic commands.
.SS Language servers on Vala projects
vala\-language\-server reads the \f[CR]command\f[R] field and ignores
the \f[CR]arguments\f[R] array; build the database with
\f[CR]format.entries.use_array_format: false\f[R].
In a mixed C/Vala project, clangd also indexes the \f[CR]valac\f[R]
entries and emits unknown\-argument noise on them; suppress it with a
\f[CR].clangd\f[R] file:
.IP
.EX
If\f[B]:\f[R]
  PathMatch\f[B]:\f[R] .*\(rs.vala
Diagnostics\f[B]:\f[R]
  Suppress\f[B]:\f[R] \(aq*\(aq
.EE
.SS Getting help
Consult the documentation site for known problems and search existing
issues before opening a new report.
Follow the bug report template, and always include the
\f[CR]RUST_LOG=debug\f[R] output.
